 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T20:24+08:00",
 "answer": "Generally speaking, because Johor Bahru and Singapore sit right next to each other across the Straits of Johor, they experience very similar regional transboundary haze conditions driven by the same Sumatra and Kalimantan forest fires. \n\nAir quality metrics in both locations fluctuate heavily depending on shifting wind directions, local showers, and hourly PM2.5 concentrations. Both regions have experienced severe spikes pushing air quality into the **\"Unhealthy\" to \"Very Unhealthy\"** ranges. \n\nBecause wind patterns can cause smoke to concentrate more densely on one side of the causeway than the other at any given hour, minor differences occur. However, neither city has a monopoly on clean air during these episodes—both Johor Bahru and Singapore face heavily polluted, hazy conditions, and residents in both areas are advised to check real-time air quality readings (such as local AQI or PSI/PM2.5 updates) before heading outdoors.",
